<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samudera Pasai Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 13th century–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Samudra Pasai, Pasai Sultanate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samudera Pasai Sultanate was a Muslim port kingdom on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhokseumawe">Lhokseumawe</a>, from the late 13th century. Its first known sultan, Malik al-Salih, died in 1297, making it one of the earliest Islamic states in maritime South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited it in the 1340s. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gold port, it was occupied by the Portuguese in 1521 and absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in 1524.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aceh grew up at the northern tip of Sumatra, where the Strait of Malacca opens onto the Indian Ocean, an area earlier dominated by the port-kingdom of Lamuri and by <b>Samudera Pasai</b>, one of the first Muslim states of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Southeast Asia Islam advanced through the port cities of the Malay world, where merchant communities and local rulers adopted it from the late thirteenth century onwards; the sultans of <b>Samudera Pasai</b> in Sumatra were among the first.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers were Muslim, following the example of nearby <b>Samudera Pasai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pedir">Pedir</a></li></ul></section>
